    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The elevator was out of order, so even though it was a cool autumn afternoon, I was sweating all over by the time I reached my seventh-floor apartment. As soon as I got inside, I headed straight for the fridge and took out a bottle of iced corn juice—my favourite drink—ready to down it in one go."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Just then, Mum came out of the bedroom and caught me red-handed. “Ah, no!” she said. “It’s not that hot today. Don’t drink something so cold. Let me pour you some hot water.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“But I’m sweating all over!” I "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "protested",
            "ref": "v-protest"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". “The elevator was broken, and I had to walk all the way up!”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“You’re always sweating, aren’t you? I’m afraid your "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "liver fire",
            "zh": "肝火"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " is too high.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Mum’s conclusion—high liver fire—reminded me of a popular meme from last summer. In one of his videos, Leizi, a social media influencer, plays both a nagging mother and her son. The mother "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "repeatedly",
            "ref": "v-repeatedly"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " pushes her son to eat "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "sponge gourd soup",
            "zh": "丝瓜汤"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " because it’s “good for your health”. When the son refuses, she complains about his bad temper. “Your liver fire must be too high,” she finally says. “Have some sponge gourd soup. It can help reduce the heat!”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "Unsurprisingly",
            "ref": "v-unsurprisingly"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", the video quickly drew millions of views. After all, who hasn’t heard lines like, “Drink more hot water”, “Wear your "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "long johns",
            "zh": "秋裤"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "”, and “Use your phone less”? Parents everywhere love to give these “for your own good” tips—and children everywhere are tired of hearing them."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "But as some netizens point out, these nagging words stand for our parents’ love for us. Many of them "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "long",
            "ref": "v-long"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to stay close to us but often find themselves too old to keep up. My mum, for example, once misunderstood my “Broken” emoji ([裂开]) on WeChat as a serious head injury. In the end, she turns to “high liver fire” to keep the conversation going."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p8",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Perhaps we should try to appreciate our parents’ “liver-fire” love. But when my “liver fire” runs high, I’d still rather drink some iced corn juice than sponge gourd soup. Next time, though, I’ll make sure to enjoy it in secret."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
